---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import DocsEnd from '../../components/DocsEnd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { t, ci } from '../../data/claims';

const crumbs = [{ label: 'Docs', href: '/' }, { label: 'Simulation', href: '/simulation/dusk/' }, { label: 'Dusk' }];
---

<DocsLayout
  crumbs={crumbs}
  code={{ name: 'dusk', repo: 'https://github.com/mruspace/dusk', language: 'Rust', license: 'Apache-2.0' }}
  active="dusk"
  toc={[
    { href: '#run', label: 'Run it' },
    { href: '#options', label: 'Options' },
    { href: '#model', label: 'The fault model' },
    { href: '#results', label: 'Default results' },
    { href: '#definitions', label: 'What the columns mean' },
    { href: '#baselines', label: 'Baselines' },
    { href: '#sweep', label: 'Sweeps' },
    { href: '#ground', label: 'Ground support' },
    { href: '#bench', label: 'Checked against the bench' },
    { href: '#claims', label: 'What this does not claim' },
  ]}
>
  <article class="body">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <h1>Dusk</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A Monte Carlo of redundancy policies on hardware that only ever decays. It asks what a shrinking quorum buys, and
      what it costs, on the same simulated hardware.
    </p>
    <div class="row" style="gap: 8px; margin: 8px 0 24px">
      <span class="pill">Rust</span>
      <span class="pill">no dependencies</span>
      <span class="pill">deterministic per seed</span>
      <a class="pill" href="https://dusk.mru.space" style="text-decoration: none">
        dusk.mru.space &#8599;
      </a>
    </div>

    <h2 id="run">Run it</h2>
    <pre
      class="pre"
      tabindex="0"
    >cargo run --release --bin dusk                 <span class="c"># the default scenario, about 2 s</span>
cargo run --release --bin dusk -- --spares 2   <span class="c"># add salvaged processors</span>
cargo run --release --bin dusk -- --sweep      <span class="c"># sensitivity and cold standby, about 2 minutes</span>
cargo run --release --bin dusk -- --ground     <span class="c"># against TMR with a ground team, about 12 s</span>
cargo run --release --bin charts               <span class="c"># redraw the charts and data.json, about 2 minutes</span></pre>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px">
      The package has two programs. <code>dusk</code> is the default, and <code>--bin charts</code> redraws the charts.
      Times are for an Apple M4 with 10 cores. Results depend only on the seed, never on the thread count.
    </p>

    <h2 id="options">Options</h2>
    <div class="tbl-box" tabindex="0">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Option</th>
            <th>Default</th>
            <th>Meaning</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--runs</td>
            <td class="m">10000</td>
            <td>Missions per policy</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--seed</td>
            <td class="m">1</td>
            <td>Base seed</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--threads</td>
            <td class="m">all cores</td>
            <td>Worker threads</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--horizon-years</td>
            <td class="m">1000</td>
            <td>Mission horizon</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--nodes</td>
            <td class="m">3</td>
            <td>Processors at launch</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--spares</td>
            <td class="m">0</td>
            <td>Salvaged processors</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--shape</td>
            <td class="m">1.5</td>
            <td>Weibull shape</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--scale-years</td>
            <td class="m">125</td>
            <td>Weibull scale</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--p-corr</td>
            <td class="m">0.1</td>
            <td>Chance a death kills another</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--p-upset</td>
            <td class="m">0.001</td>
            <td>Bad-result upsets per node-day</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--coverage</td>
            <td class="m">0.99</td>
            <td>Lone self-check coverage</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--selfcheck-throughput</td>
            <td class="m">0.5</td>
            <td>Lone output vs a vote</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">--cold-aging</td>
            <td class="m">0.1 and 1.0</td>
            <td>
              How fast an unpowered spare ages, relative to a powered one. The report shows both ends; give a value to
              show one.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 id="model">The fault model</h2>
    <p>Each mission is a day-by-day fault-injection run.</p>
    <ul class="sig-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Processors die</strong> on a Weibull lifetime. Shape 1.5 and a 125-year scale give a median life of
        about 98 years.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Deaths can be correlated.</strong> With probability <code>p_corr</code>, a death also kills one other
        processor that day.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Spares are salvaged</strong> from instruments as they die, already aged by the same radiation.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Upsets corrupt a day&#39;s result</strong>, at the rate that gets past EDAC and scrubbing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The comparison is paired.</strong> Every policy sees the same fault history, so any difference is the
        policy.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="results">Default results</h2>
    <pre
      class="pre"
      tabindex="0"
    >policy                     useful      p10      p50      p90      service   wrong days     risk
                           mean y                                  mean y   mean / run   P(&gt;=1)
fixed TMR                    99.0     37.6     92.7    168.9         99.0        0.000     0.0%
standby simplex              83.1     38.4     78.3    134.0        166.4        0.603    43.2%
shrinking quorum            <span class="s">132.6     64.3    126.6    208.3</span>        166.4        0.246    20.1%
TMR + unchecked simplex     166.2     76.9    156.7    267.9        166.4       24.715    87.2%
cold standby, aging 0.1     144.7     69.7    139.3    226.7        289.8        1.043    61.3%
cold standby, aging 1.0      83.1     38.4     78.3    134.0        166.4        0.603    43.2%</pre>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px">
      {t('dusk.work_vs_tmr')} the useful work of fixed TMR in total (95% CI {ci('dusk.work_vs_tmr')}). This assumes no
      ground support after launch. The gain falls to {t('dusk.ground.never')} when support never ends. See{' '}
      <a href="#ground">Ground support</a>. Never less on any single mission, by construction: the two policies are
      identical until fixed TMR stops. Fixed TMR comes out ahead only if one wrong result costs more than{' '}
      {t('dusk.break_even_years')} years of useful work (95% CI {ci('dusk.break_even_years')}), next to a risk of{' '}
      {t('dusk.risk.shrink')} against {t('dusk.risk.tmr')}.
    </p>
    <p>
      Cold standby is the nearest model here of dual-string with autonomous switchover. Standby simplex is the same
      design with spares that age as fast as a powered processor, so it equals cold standby at aging 1.0. See{' '}
      <a href="#baselines">Baselines</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="definitions">What the columns mean</h2>
    <p>
      Each mission runs one day at a time. On each day the policy runs in one mode, set by the number of live
      processors, and the lowest-numbered live processors do the work. These are the definitions in{' '}
      <code>src/model.rs</code>.
    </p>
    <ul class="sig-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Useful.</strong> The sum over days of throughput credit for a correct delivered result: 1.0 for a vote
        day with at most one upset, 1.0 for a pair day with no upset, 0.5 (<code>--selfcheck-throughput</code>) for a
        single self-check day with no upset, 1.0 for an unchecked day with no upset, and 0 for lost, wrong and idle
        days. Shown as the mean in years, with the 10th, 50th and 90th percentiles.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Service.</strong> The first day the policy can never produce output again, or the horizon. For fixed
        TMR, the first day with fewer than two live processors. For the others, the first day with none left. The model
        ends the run that day, so a spare due to join later is not used. Shown as the mean in years.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Wrong days.</strong> Days whose delivered result was corrupted by an upset that nothing caught. One day
        is one result. Only a lone processor can make one: its self-check misses an upset with probability 1 minus{' '}
        <code>--coverage</code>, and an unchecked processor delivers every upset. Shown as the mean per mission.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Risk.</strong> The share of missions with at least one wrong day, with a paired bootstrap 95% CI.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px">
      A vote day with two or more upsets and a pair day with any upset are lost: the policy catches the disagreement and
      delivers nothing.
    </p>

    <h2 id="baselines">Baselines</h2>
    <p>
      Fixed TMR is one baseline, not what most spacecraft fly. Two more act with no ground team, on the same fault
      histories.
    </p>
    <ul class="sig-list">
      <li>
        <strong>TMR + unchecked simplex</strong> steps down on its own, but its last processor does not check itself. It
        isolates the value of self-check. The shrinking quorum does {t('dusk.work_vs_unchecked')} of its useful work and
        makes {t('dusk.wrong_vs_unchecked')} of its wrong results (95% CI {ci('dusk.wrong_vs_unchecked')}). Risk:{' '}
        {t('dusk.risk.unchecked')} against {t('dusk.risk.shrink')}.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cold standby</strong> keeps one processor powered and switches on the next, in order, when it dies. The
        active one self-checks. Here it has three strings, on the same hardware as TMR. Correlated deaths follow the
        powered fault history, which slightly under-counts them for an unpowered spare.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px">
      How fast an unpowered spare ages is not known, so <code>--sweep</code> varies it from 0.1 to 1.0 of the powered
      rate. Against cold standby, the shrinking quorum does {t('dusk.work_vs_cold.01')} the useful work at 0.1 (95% CI{' '}
      {ci('dusk.work_vs_cold.01')}) and {t('dusk.work_vs_cold.10')} at 1.0 (95% CI {ci('dusk.work_vs_cold.10')}). Cold
      standby does more useful work below an aging rate of about {t('dusk.cold.crossing')}. At every rate it has more
      missions with a wrong result: {t('dusk.risk.cold_10')} to {t('dusk.risk.cold_01')}, against{' '}
      {t('dusk.risk.shrink')}. With two strings (<code>--nodes 2</code>), the shrinking quorum does{' '}
      {t('dusk.nodes2.work_vs_cold.01')} to {t('dusk.nodes2.work_vs_cold.10')} the useful work of cold standby.
    </p>

    <h2 id="sweep">Sweeps</h2>
    <p>
      <code>--sweep</code> varies each parameter the model cannot pin down. Across every row, the gain over fixed TMR is{' '}
      {t('dusk.sweep.range')}. Every row assumes no ground support after launch.
    </p>

    <h2 id="ground">Ground support</h2>
    <p>
      The 1.34× above assumes no ground support after launch. <code>--ground</code> compares the shrinking quorum with
      fixed TMR plus a ground team. When TMR drops to one processor, the team commands it into self-check after an
      answer time, if support has not ended. The gain falls as support lasts longer:
    </p>
    <pre class="pre" tabindex="0">10000 missions, seed 1; shrinking quorum&#39;s useful work vs TMR + ground fallback

ground support ends        1 day   30 days  180 days   95% CI at 30 days
at launch                  1.34x     1.34x     1.34x   1.33x to 1.35x
after 10 years             1.33x     1.33x     1.33x   1.33x to 1.34x
after 25 years             1.31x     1.31x     1.31x   1.30x to 1.32x
after 50 years             1.25x     1.25x     1.25x   1.24x to 1.25x
after 100 years            1.11x     1.11x     1.11x   1.10x to 1.11x
after 200 years            1.01x     1.01x     1.01x   1.01x to 1.01x
never                      1.00x     1.00x     1.00x   1.00x to 1.00x</pre>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px">
      With support that never ends, the gain is 1.00×: the ground does by hand what the shrinking quorum does alone.
      With 49 years of support, Voyager&#39;s so far, the charts program gives 1.25×. Answer time barely matters. What
      matters is whether anyone is still there.
    </p>

    <h2 id="bench">Checked against the bench</h2>
    <p>
      <code>--bench FILE</code> replays the schedules of the <a href="/core/bench/">Layer 1 bench</a> through the model
      and compares the means. On the published results, 17 of 18 rows agree within 3 standard errors. Details are on the{' '}
      <a href="/core/bench/#model">bench page</a>. This checks the policy model against the real code. It does not
      calibrate the environment.
    </p>

    <h2 id="claims">What this does not claim</h2>
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="tiny">Note</span>
      <span>
        The parameters are illustrative, not calibrated. The claim is the shape of the trade, and the sweep shows where
        it holds.
      </span>
    </div>
    <DocsEnd />
  </article>
</DocsLayout>
